.TH "TTYD-CLIENT" "1" "October 2026" "ttyd-client" "User Manual"
.SH NAME
ttyd-client \- connect a terminal to a ttyd server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ttyd-client
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIurl\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ttyd-client
is a command line client for
.BR ttyd (1)
(share your terminal over the web). It connects to the websocket endpoint of a
ttyd server and attaches the local terminal to the remote session, so that a
shared terminal can be used from a shell exactly like
.BR ssh (1),
without a web browser.
.PP
The client speaks the same protocol as the ttyd web frontend: it fetches the
authentication token from the
.I /token
endpoint, opens the
.I /ws
websocket, sends the initial
.B JSON_DATA
message with the terminal size, and then exchanges
.BR INPUT " / " OUTPUT " / " RESIZE_TERMINAL " / " PAUSE " / " RESUME
messages. Window titles and client preferences sent by the server are applied
to the local terminal.
.PP
Features:
.IP \(bu 2
Pure C, statically linked OpenSSL for TLS (wss/https)
.IP \(bu 2
Same protocol and server options as the ttyd web client
.IP \(bu 2
Basic authentication, auth proxy headers and URL arguments
.IP \(bu 2
Terminal resize forwarding, window title updates and flow control
.IP \(bu 2
Automatic reconnection with exponential backoff
.IP \(bu 2
HTTP proxy (CONNECT) and TLS (wss) support
.IP \(bu 2
Statically linkable, cross platform: Linux (glibc/musl), macOS, FreeBSD/OpenBSD, OpenWrt, Windows
.SH URLS
The url argument accepts the following forms:
.TP
.B host
.B host:port
A plain host name or address. The connection uses
.B ws
unless
.B \-\-ssl
is given, and the default port is 7681.
.TP
.B ws://host:port/path
.B wss://host:port/path
The websocket endpoint, or the base path of the server. The default ports are
80 and 443.
.TP
.B http://host:port/path
.B https://host:port/path
The base path of a ttyd server. The
.I /ws
and
.I /token
endpoints are derived from it.
.PP
When the path ends with
.I /ws
or
.I /token
it is stripped to obtain the base path first, so all of these are equivalent
for a server listening on port 7681:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd-client localhost:7681
    ttyd-client ws://localhost:7681/ws
    ttyd-client http://localhost:7681/
.fi
.PP
For a server started with
.BR "ttyd \-b /mounted/here" ,
use any of:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd-client http://localhost:7681/mounted/here/
    ttyd-client ws://localhost:7681/mounted/here/ws
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-port " <port>"
Port to connect to, overriding the port from the url.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-credential " <username:password>"
Credential for basic authentication. The token is fetched from the
.I /token
endpoint and sent in the
.B Authorization
header and in the initial
.B AuthToken
field, exactly like the web client does.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-\-auth-header " <name=value>"
Extra HTTP header sent with the token request and the websocket handshake.
Useful when ttyd runs behind a reverse proxy that authenticates requests (the
.B \-\-auth-header
server option). Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-url\-arg " <key=value>"
Append an argument to the websocket url. Requires the server to run with
.BR \-\-url\-arg ,
where it becomes an extra command line argument of the shared command.
Repeatable. A value without
.B =
is sent as
.BR arg=<value> .
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-client\-option " <key=value>"
Override a client preference sent by the server, for example
.BR titleFixed=ttyd-client .
The value is parsed as JSON when possible, otherwise it is used as a string.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-writable
Send keyboard input to the remote terminal (this is the default).
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-readonly
Do not send keyboard input, only display the remote terminal.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-eof\-action " <eot|quit|ignore>"
What to do when stdin reaches end of file: send a
.B ^D
character (default), quit, or keep the session open. The
.B ^D
(and the quit) is deferred by 200 ms so that it does not arrive in the middle of
a command that was piped in on the last line, which keeps constructs such as
.I printf 'ls' | ttyd-client host
working.
.TP
.B \-\-escape\-char <char|none>
Local character used to detach from the session without disturbing the remote
command. The default is
.B ^]
(0x1d). Use
.B none
to disable it.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reconnect " <n>"
Maximum number of reconnect attempts after a connection failure. Use
.B \-1
for unlimited attempts. The default is 5.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-reconnect
Never reconnect. Equivalent to
.BR "\-\-reconnect 0" .
.TP
.B \-\-reconnect\-on\-eof
Also reconnect when the server ends the session, which happens when the remote
command exits. This matches the behaviour of the browser client, which shows
"Press Enter to Reconnect" instead of closing the page.
.TP
.B \-\-reconnect\-delay <ms>
Initial delay between reconnect attempts in milliseconds (default: 1000). The
delay doubles after every failed attempt.
.TP
.B \-\-reconnect\-max\-delay <ms>
Upper bound for the reconnect delay in milliseconds (default: 30000).
.TP
.B \-\-connect\-timeout <seconds>
Timeout for the TCP connect, the TLS handshake and the websocket upgrade
(default: 15, 0 disables the timeout).
.TP
.BR \-P ", " \-\-ping\-interval " <seconds>"
Interval of the client side websocket keepalive pings (default: 5, 0 disables
them). The ttyd server closes idle connections after about 10 seconds, so
pongs are always answered even when pings are disabled.
.TP
.B \-\-flow\-limit <bytes>
Number of output bytes written between two flow control checks (default:
100000), mirroring the web client.
.TP
.B \-\-flow\-high\-water <bytes>
Output backlog size that makes the client send a
.B PAUSE
message to the server (default: 1048576).
.TP
.B \-\-flow\-low\-water <bytes>
Output backlog size that makes the client send a
.B RESUME
message again (default: 262144).
.TP
.B \-\-cols <columns>, \-\-rows <rows>
Initial terminal size. By default the size of the local terminal is used.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resize
Never send
.B RESIZE_TERMINAL
messages, even when the local terminal is resized.
.TP
.B \-\-title <title>
Use a fixed local window title instead of the one sent by the server.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-title
Never change the local window title.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-ssl
Use TLS (wss) for urls that do not contain a scheme.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-insecure
Do not verify the server certificate.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-ssl\-cert " <path>"
Client certificate (PEM) used when the server requests one.
.TP
.BR \-K ", " \-\-ssl\-key " <path>"
Private key (PEM) belonging to the client certificate.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-ssl\-ca " <path>"
CA bundle used to verify the server certificate, instead of the platform
trust store. On Windows the platform store is the Windows certificate store
(current user and local machine
.BR ROOT );
elsewhere it is the OpenSSL default CA file and directory.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-proxy " <url>"
HTTP proxy used for the connection (format:
.IR http://[user:password@]host[:port] ).
By default the
.BR https_proxy ", " http_proxy " or " all_proxy
environment variables are used.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-proxy
Never use a proxy.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Only print errors.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-debug " <level>"
Log level bitmask, using the same values as
.BR "ttyd \-\-debug" :
1 error, 2 warning, 4 notice, 8 info, 16 debug, 1024 timestamps. The default is
6 (errors, warnings and notices). Use 31 to also see debug and info messages.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help text and exit.
.SH CLIENT OPTIONS
The server can send client preferences with the
.B SET_PREFERENCES
message (the
.B \-\-client\-option
option of ttyd, or the query string of a browser url). ttyd-client understands
the following keys:
.TP
.B titleFixed
Use this string as a fixed window title.
.TP
.B disableReconnect, closeOnDisconnect
Disable the automatic reconnection.
.TP
.B disableStdin
Stop forwarding keyboard input.
.TP
.B disableTitle
Do not change the window title.
.PP
All other keys are ignored but shown at debug level. Local
.B \-\-client\-option
values take precedence over the values sent by the server.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B Ctrl\-]
Detach from the session (configurable with
.BR \-\-escape\-char ).
The websocket is closed cleanly; whether the remote command keeps running
depends on the server options (
.BR \-\-once / \-\-exit\-no\-conn
terminate it, a plain server keeps it alive until the client reconnects).
.PP
Every other key sequence is forwarded to the remote terminal. While the local
terminal is in raw mode Ctrl-C is passed to the remote shell, so use
.B Ctrl\-]
or a signal to leave the client.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B https_proxy, HTTPS_PROXY, http_proxy, HTTP_PROXY, all_proxy, ALL_PROXY
Proxy used for the connection when
.B \-\-proxy
is not given. Ignored with
.BR \-\-no\-proxy .
.TP
.B COLUMNS, LINES
Fallback terminal size when it cannot be queried.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The session ended normally, either because the server closed it with the
websocket code 1000 (the remote command exited successfully), because the
remote terminal was closed, or because the user pressed
.BR Ctrl\-] .
.TP
.B 1
Connection, authentication or protocol error, an abnormal close code, or no
remaining reconnect attempts.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command line usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start a server and attach to it:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd \-W bash
    ttyd-client localhost:7681
.fi
.PP
Connect to a password protected server mounted behind a reverse proxy:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd-client \-c user:pass https://example.com/terminal/
.fi
.PP
Watch a session without being able to type into it:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd-client \-\-readonly wss://example.com/terminal/ws
.fi
.PP
Pass extra command line arguments to the remote command:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd \-W \-a bash                  # server side
    ttyd-client \-a top localhost:7681 # runs: bash top
.fi
.PP
Trust a private certificate authority:
.PP
.nf
    ttyd-client \-A ./ca.pem wss://localhost:7681/ws
.fi
.SH PROTOCOL
Every websocket message is a binary frame whose first byte is the command
character, followed by the payload.
.PP
.nf
    client -> server        server -> client
    0 INPUT                0 OUTPUT
    1 RESIZE_TERMINAL      1 SET_WINDOW_TITLE
    2 PAUSE                2 SET_PREFERENCES
    3 RESUME
    { JSON_DATA (AuthToken, columns, rows)
.fi
.PP
The initial
.B JSON_DATA
message starts the remote process and must be the first message sent by the
client. The terminal size is part of it as well as of
.BR RESIZE_TERMINAL ,
in the form
.BR {"columns":W,"rows":H} .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ttyd (1),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR lrzsz (1)
.PP
Full documentation and the source code:
.IR https://github.com/tsl0922/ttyd
